title: Confession and Forgiveness – Doctrine of Assurance
description: The saved confess their sins to the Father, who is faithful to forgive: confession restores fellowship, and the believer is never lost. From the teachings on 1 John 1, Psalm 51 and Proverbs 28.
output: lessons/doctrine/topic-assurance-confession-and-forgiveness.html

# Built from the "Confession and forgiveness" teachings in studies/data/topics/assurance.json, in the author's words.

=== Introduction
# from 18022
--- Confession and Forgiveness [title]
overline: Doctrine · Assurance
heading: Confession and Forgiveness
sub: God wants to forgive us more than we want to be forgiven.
refs: 1 John 1:9; Proverbs 28:13; Psalm 51:12
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move through the study. Tap any gold reference to read the verse.

# from 1677, 17387
--- Faithful and just to forgive
big: 1 John 1:9
lead: First John, the epistle, is written to the saved.
p: Here the Bible tells us if we the saved, want fellowship with God and one another, to walk with God, to confess our sins to Him, and be forgiven them all by Christ, washed clean in His blood.
callout: We know that in Christ our sins are forgiven, past, present and future. But we are also to faithfully confess our sins to the Father, Who is faithful again to forgive.

=== Why we confess
# from 11083
--- Admitting our sin before God
lead: Confession, the act of admitting guilt, of revealing your own faults and iniquities, is a fundamental act of humility in the Christian life.
p: A soul that sins and doesn't confess their trespass to God, can't avail themselves of the sacrifice. You can't get saved without this acknowledgment. You can't accept salvation without knowing you need to be saved.
callout: And so we must confess we are guilty under the law to be saved, and once saved, we confess our sins that God may work through the Spirit indwelling us to remove those thorns in our hearts.

# from 11083, 1728
--- Pride against confession
panel-warn: Works religion | This pride against confession is what draws people to a works-based false religion. People seek to be absolved in any way possible short of actually acknowledging their sin.
panel-warn: “Perfect contrition” | It requires the sinner to be 100% honest, 100% contrite, about 100% of their sins, that depends on the sinner alone, not the blood of Christ.
panel: The believer | We confess our sins to God knowing He's forgiven them already, that we have eternal grace that's never lost or taken away, in Christ Jesus.
callout: Working to maintain God's grace makes grace no more grace. [[Romans 11:6]]

# from 1677
--- If we say that we have no sin
scripture: 1 John 1:8-10
panel-warn: Sinless perfection | But some claim sinless perfection, and believe what saves them is their not sinning. According to the Bible they are liars.

# from 18021
--- Our flesh sins, not the new man
big: 1 John 3:9
p: John is not contradicting himself by saying if we say we have no sin, we are liars and simultaneously saying we cannot sin, being born of God.
lead: When we sin, it is not the new man, created in Christ, but the flesh.
callout: This promise shows not only the faithfulness of God to forgive, but also that our sin in the flesh is not held against our ledger in Heaven. It is not imputed against those that are in Christ.
refs: Romans 7:15-17; Galatians 5:24-25

# from 1677
--- Written to the brethren
scripture: 1 John 2:1-2
p: Note he is writing to brethren. To the children of God, babes in Christ. He is edifying them and encouraging them to walk with God, to avoid sin and not deny it, but understand that if we are honest we confess our sins, and they are forgiven already in Christ.
center: **Because we are sons of God.** [[1 John 2:12]]

=== David's confession
# from 18011, 14249
--- David's cry of contrition
kicker: Psalm 51 · 2 Samuel 12
lead: Psalm 51 is the clearest and most sober cry of contrition and supplication for forgiveness by a believer, in the face of a judgment by the Almighty God, that we have in Scripture.
big: 2 Samuel 12:13
p: David acknowledges his sin and understands his plight is of his own making, and ultimately accepts God's judgment against him. This is a model for us to follow. This is how we get right with God.

# from 18012
--- Against thee only have I sinned
scripture: Psalm 51:3-4
panel-warn: Bitterness | These people are destroyed with their bitterness, wracked with guilt, have a broken relationship with the God that bought them, and are often called home early, the result of their being incapable of being honest and appealing to His mercy.
panel: David | David acknowledges his understanding that God's judgments are right and deserved. But David shows us the better way.

# from 18022
--- Restore unto me the joy of thy salvation
big: Psalm 51:11-12
p: David is not writing that he is afraid to lose his salvation, but seeking the confidence in it again that has been marred by his transgressions and the fear from the chastening he has received.
p: He is seeking to renew his relationship with God through his confession and appeals to mercy.
callout: It is the mercy God shows us as His children that leads us to share His praise to others. [[Psalm 51:13]]

=== Covering or confessing
# from 17387, 15475
--- He that covereth his sins
big: Proverbs 28:13
lead: Covering sin leads to the chastening of the Lord. Humbling ourselves to admit our failings will have mercy.
p: Don't deceive yourself when faced with chastisement of the Lord. God is long-suffering and merciful to a humble heart, calling out to Him. But you forsake mercy when you stay in denial.
callout: Confession, telling the truth in our heart about what it is we have done, what we do, is for our sake; that we may know His mercy and forgiveness and be cleaned from all unrighteousness.

# from 7252, 18022
--- Forgiveness is not earned by penance
panel-warn: Penance | God doesn't want sacrifice. He doesn't want our works of penance and vows to change. He takes no delight in the ritual formulas of burnt offerings, and performative acts of contrition.
panel: Grace | Penance isn't how we earn forgiveness. Forgiveness is given by grace and mercy, charitable love extended to a sincere change of heart and contrition, acknowledging what we've done and how it impacted others.
refs: Psalm 51:16-17; Isaiah 1:18; Isaiah 55:7

# from 18022, 7252
--- The Father ran
scripture: Luke 15:18-20 | mark: his father saw him, and had compassion, and ran
p: The Father runs to meet his returning son, showering him with love. Before any confession of guilt or offer of penance or anything other than turning back towards God, the Father goes to him in compassion and restitution of their relationship.
callout: God wants to forgive us more than we want to be forgiven.

=== The liberty of the forgiven
# from 17368, 8691
--- Bold as a lion
kicker: Proverbs 28:1
big: Hebrews 4:16
p: This boldness comes from having a clean conscience towards God by the blood of Christ.
p: We don't need to keep a guilty conscience, in constant anxiety. We have the boldness of Christ's righteousness and the promise of a merciful Father.
callout: We shouldn't be miserable. We shouldn't cling to guilt for sins and iniquities God has forgiven and remembers no more.
refs: Proverbs 28:1; Hebrews 10:19-22

# from 8698
--- New every morning
big: Lamentations 3:22-23
lead: No matter how backslidden. No matter how snared in sin. No matter how the devil accuses you before God, in Christ Jesus you are forgiven.
p: For a saved believer who has been sealed by the Spirit, it is never too late to turn back to God and away from your sins. Like the Prodigal son, returning to the Father.
refs: Psalm 103:12; Isaiah 43:25; Isaiah 38:17; Hebrews 10:17; Romans 11:29

# from 11084, 16591
--- Confess your faults one to another
big: James 5:16
p: Brothers and sisters in Christ should edify one another, being of one mind in Christ.
panel: True confession | True confession is a wonderful tool given by God that we may become separated unto the Gospel and better vessels fitted to His purpose.
panel-warn: False confession | False confession, anything that seeks to be given excuse or justification for our sin that we may remain in it, or that we can work it off in some way of penance, is wicked as Hell and leads people further and further into sin.

=== Summary
# from 1677, 11083, 18021, 18011, 17387, 7252, 8698
--- In summary
point: Written to the saved | If we the saved want fellowship with God, we confess our sins to Him, and they are forgiven already in Christ.
point: Confession is humility | Once saved, we confess our sins that God may work through the Spirit indwelling us.
point: The flesh sins | When we sin, it is not the new man, created in Christ, but the flesh. It is not imputed against those that are in Christ.
point: David's way | He acknowledged his sin and accepted God's judgment. This is how we get right with God.
point: Covering or confessing | Covering sin leads to the chastening of the Lord. Humbling ourselves to admit our failings will have mercy.
point: Not penance | Forgiveness is given by grace and mercy. His mercies are new every morning.

